WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
Dependency management requires knowing what software the application uses and where it is

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:05.940
deployed.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.680
Without inventory,

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.980
teams cannot reliably assess exposure when a new vulnerability is announced.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.760
Unsupported software is difficult to defend because fixes may no longer be available.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.740
Legacy frameworks, old runtimes, abandoned packages,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.200
and unmaintained plugins can become permanent risk unless they are replaced.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.980
Third-party risk includes both known vulnerabilities and the possibility of supply chain

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.240
compromise.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.740
Packages, build tools, images,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.520
and vendor components should be evaluated as part of the application security boundary.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.100
Version updates must be paired with testing and release discipline.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.440
The goal is to remediate exploitable risk quickly without creating instability that causes

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.820
teams to delay patching indefinitely.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.560
Maintain software inventory and dependency visibility.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.500
Use vulnerability monitoring and dependency scanning.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.800
Patch, upgrade, or isolate vulnerable components according to risk.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.380
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-2 - Flaw Remediation;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:14.000
CM-8 - System Component Inventory; RA-5 - Vulnerability Monitoring and Scanning.
